@page "/docs/primitives/composition"

<DocsPage Title="Composition" Description="A guide to composing the primitives with your own components.">
    <DocsHeading Title="Composing your own components" />
    <p>
        Every part that renders an element takes a <code>Render</code> fragment. The part gives the fragment its attributes (the id, role, ARIA and <code>data-*</code>
        attributes, and its event handlers) as <code>Attributes</code>, and you spread them onto whatever you render.
    </p>
    <p>For example, most triggers render a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> by default. Render your own button component instead:</p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@CustomButtonCode" Title="Song.razor" />
    <p>
        A component you render must pass the attributes on to its root element, usually with a parameter that has
        <code>CaptureUnmatchedValues</code>:
    </p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@ForwardCode" Title="MyButton.razor" />
    <p>Follow these rules:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Spread <code>Attributes</code> onto the focusable element.</strong> The keyboard and focus handling look for
            it by the id the attributes carry.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Name each <code>Context</code> when you nest them</strong> (<code>Context="trigger"</code>,
            <code>Context="item"</code>...). Blazor's default name, <code>context</code>, collides with itself.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>You don't set <code>NativeButton</code>.</strong> A <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>
            trigger needs <code>role="button"</code>, a tab stop, Enter and Space, and <code>aria-disabled</code>, and a real
            <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> must not get them. The browser
            reports the element after the first interactive render, and the part renders again to match. Set it only to skip
            that check, so prerendered HTML is right from the first byte.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <DocsHeading Title="Composing several parts" />
    <p>
        A <code>Render</code> fragment can hold another part, as deep as you need. A button that's a tooltip trigger, a dialog
        trigger and a menu trigger at once is a common case:
    </p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@NestedCode" Title="Toolbar.razor" />

    <DocsHeading Title="How attributes are merged" />
    <p>
        Attributes you put on a part are merged into the <code>Attributes</code> it hands its <code>Render</code> fragment:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Your <code>class</code>, <code>data-*</code> and other attributes are kept, unless the part sets the same one.</li>
        <li>Your event handlers run before the part's own, and both run.</li>
        <li>Your <code>style</code> comes after the part's, so your declarations win.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        An attribute you write after the spread on your own element replaces the part's. Parameters such as
        <code>Disabled</code> are read by each part on its own, so one part's parameter doesn't change another's.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>AttributeBuilder</code> does this merging. Use it, with <a href="/docs/primitives/use-render"><code>UseRender</code></a>,
        to give parts of your own the same <code>Render</code> support.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Changing the default element" />
    <p>
        <code>Render</code> also replaces the element a part renders. For example, <code>AccordionHeader</code> renders an
        <code>&lt;h3&gt;</code>; render an <code>&lt;h2&gt;</code> where the page's heading levels need one:
    </p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@HeadingCode" Title="Faq.razor" />
    <p>
        Each part renders the most fitting element by default, so render a different one only when you have a reason to.
    </p>

    <DocsHeading Title="Rendering from state" />
    <p>
        The fragment's props also carry the part's state, such as <code>Open</code> on a trigger, or
        <code>Highlighted</code>, <code>Checked</code> and <code>Disabled</code> on items. Use it to render different
        content for each state:
    </p>
    <CodeFigure Code="@StateCode" Title="Switch.razor" />
</DocsPage>

@code {
    private const string CustomButtonCode = """
        <MenuTrigger>
            <Render Context="trigger">
                <MyButton @attributes="trigger.Attributes" Size="md">Open menu</MyButton>
            </Render>
        </MenuTrigger>
        """;

    private const string ForwardCode = """
        <button class="@($"my-button {Size}")" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">@ChildContent</button>

        @code {
            [Parameter] public string Size { get; set; } = "md";
            [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
            [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)] public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }
        }
        """;

    private const string NestedCode = """
        <DialogRoot>
            <TooltipRoot>
                <TooltipTrigger>
                    <Render Context="tooltip">
                        <DialogTrigger @attributes="tooltip.Attributes">
                            <Render Context="dialog">
                                <MenuTrigger @attributes="dialog.Attributes">
                                    <Render Context="menu">
                                        <MyButton @attributes="menu.Attributes" Size="md">Open menu</MyButton>
                                    </Render>
                                </MenuTrigger>
                            </Render>
                        </DialogTrigger>
                    </Render>
                </TooltipTrigger>
                <TooltipPopup>...</TooltipPopup>
            </TooltipRoot>
            <DialogPopup>...</DialogPopup>
        </DialogRoot>
        """;

    private const string HeadingCode = """
        <AccordionRoot>
            <AccordionItem>
                <AccordionHeader>
                    <Render Context="header">
                        <h2 @attributes="header.Attributes">
                            <AccordionTrigger>What is Blazix.Primitives?</AccordionTrigger>
                        </h2>
                    </Render>
                </AccordionHeader>
                <AccordionPanel>Blazix.Primitives is a library of unstyled Blazor components.</AccordionPanel>
            </AccordionItem>
        </AccordionRoot>
        """;

    private const string StateCode = """
        <SwitchThumb>
            <Render Context="thumb">
                <span @attributes="thumb.Attributes">
                    @(thumb.Checked ? "On" : "Off")
                </span>
            </Render>
        </SwitchThumb>
        """;
}
